on the whole domain.","An instantaneous rate of change from one direction","The largest value attained by the function near the point.","Whether the function is bounded on every interval."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bc760a781000dd295a012ac5"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_derivative_70a3d191_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x) = |x|$, what is the right derivative at $0$?","options":["$-1$.","$0$.","$1$.","$2$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f576b856d95cf3ad17838bc4"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_derivative_70a3d191_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x) = |x|$, what is the left derivative at $0$?","options":["$-1$.","$1$.","$0$.","$2$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"52002e52d41ac8502a960b38"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_derivative_70a3d191_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If both one-sided derivatives exist at $x_0$, what additional condition implies differentiability at $x_0$?","options":["The left derivative is negative.","The right derivative is positive.","The left and right derivatives are equal.","The function is defined only on one side of $x_0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8fb1d37cd9921e0ec28c5048"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_derivative_70a3d191_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Is a one-sided derivative the same object as a one-sided limit of the derivative function?","options":["They are always the same object by definition.","One-sided derivatives only exist when the derivative function is continuous.","A one-sided derivative is not the same as a one-sided limit of the derivative function.","A one-sided derivative never uses a limit."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3675504082f1534bdfadba6d"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_derivative_70a3d191_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x) = x$ for $x\\ \\ge 0$ and $f(x) = 2x$ for $x \\lt 0$. What can be concluded at $0$?","options":["$f'_+(0) = 2$, $f'_-(0) = 1$, so $f$ is differentiable at $0$.","$f'_+(0) = f'_-(0) = 1$, so $f$ is differentiable at $0$.","Neither one-sided derivative exists.","$f'_+(0) = 1$, $f'_-(0) = 2$, so $f$ is not differentiable at $0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0e6f8c7ada2a2731aaedede8"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_derivative_70a3d191_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x) = x^2$ for $x\\ \\ge 0$ and consider the boundary point $0$ of the domain $[0,\\infty)$. What is $f'_+(0)$?","options":["$1$.","$-1$.","$0$.","$2$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"63fdc5d5d2a4b145f23684dd"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_derivative_70a3d191_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the left derivative use the same quotient $\\frac{f(x)-f(x_0)}{x-x_0}$ rather than changing the denominator to $x_0-x$?","options":["Because $x-x_0$ is always positive from the left.","The quotient is kept; only the limit direction changes","Because the left derivative ignores the value $f(x_0)$.","Because the left derivative is defined without a limit."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ff9f813e824f4b7a61faaf95"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_derivative_70a3d191_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which situation is especially suited to a one-sided derivative?","options":["A point where no function value is specified.","A point outside the domain.","A boundary point where the function is defined only on one side.","A point where the independent variable is not real."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4fc5030baf181e0b4ec27b98"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_derivative_70a3d191_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x) = x^2$ for $x\\ \\ge 0$ and $f(x) = x$ for $x \\lt 0$, with $f(0) = 0$. Which statement is true at $0$?","options":["$f'_+(0) = 0$ and $f'_-(0) = 1$, so $f$ is not differentiable at $0$.","$f'_+(0) = 1$ and $f'_-(0) = 0$, so $f$ is differentiable at $0$.","$f'_+(0) = f'_-(0) = 0$, so $f$ is differentiable at $0$.","The one-sided derivatives do not exist because $f(0) = 0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"863fda0333a84157b4ec9622"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_derivative_70a3d191_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A function has $f'_+(a) = 4$ and $f'_-(a) = 4$. What follows?","options":["$f$ is differentiable at $a$ and $f'(a) = 4$.","$f$ is not differentiable at $a$ because the derivatives are one-sided.","$f$ must be a polynomial near $a$.","$f$ has a corner at $a$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"12b69b25064a66170024e691"},{"id":"one_sided_derivative_new_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which notation denotes the left derivative at $x_0$?","options":["$f'_+(x_0)$","$f'_-(x_0)$","$f(x_0^+)$","$f^{-1}(x_0)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d0478c50d68675906c6dcbdc"},{"id":"one_sided_derivative_new_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which notation denotes the right derivative at $x_0$?","options":["$f'_+(x_0)$","$f'_-(x_0)$","$f(x_0^-)$","$\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}f(x)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"211838d82e635494faf19850"},{"id":"one_sided_derivative_new_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What quotient is used for both one-sided derivatives?","options":["$\\dfrac{f(x_0)-f(x)}{x-x_0}$","$\\dfrac{f(x)}{x_0}$","$\\dfrac{f(x)-f(x_0)}{x-x_0}$","$\\dfrac{x-x_0}{f(x)-f(x_0)}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5f9dd0f7cab8fbff7af0ce57"},{"id":"one_sided_derivative_new_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=3x$ for $x\\ge0$. What is $f'_+(0)$?","options":["$0$","$-3$","$3$","The right derivative is undefined"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"416de02d31ae2578401ea90e"},{"id":"one_sided_derivative_new_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=-x$ for $x\\le0$ and $f(0)=0$. What is $f'_-(0)$?","options":["$-1$","$1$","$0$","The left derivative is undefined"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a069ad68b2431b7a12936c5f"},{"id":"one_sided_derivative_new_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=x^2$ for $x\\le1$. What is the left derivative at $1$?","options":["$1$","$0$","$2$","$-2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"08c1861cdcf582f29378048b"},{"id":"one_sided_derivative_new_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why can both one-sided derivatives exist without ordinary differentiability?","options":["They must always be equal","Ordinary differentiability ignores slopes","One-sided derivatives are not limits","They may have different values"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0f5479728e5f56987148ab1e"},{"id":"one_sided_derivative_new_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f'_-(a)=2$ and $f'_+(a)=5$. What follows?","options":["$f'(a)=7$, the sum of the slopes","$f'(a)=2$","$f'(a)=5$","$f$ is not differentiable at $a$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ca815cdd3c0a5cc48193f9db"}]}}